    {
      "id": "a2.g02.l3", "title": "Irregular participles: thinking and moving", "kind": "grammar",
      "explain": [
        { "type": "p", "text": "Here are more irregular participles. A few end in **-cht** (*gekocht, gebracht, gedacht, gezocht*). Remember that **gaan, komen, blijven, worden, beginnen, vertrekken** and **vallen** use **zijn**." },
        { "type": "table", "head": ["infinitive", "participle", "infinitive", "participle"], "rows": [["vinden", "gevonden", "kopen", "gekocht"], ["brengen", "gebracht", "denken", "gedacht"], ["zoeken", "gezocht", "zeggen", "gezegd"], ["weten", "geweten", "krijgen", "gekregen"], ["houden", "gehouden", "lopen", "gelopen"], ["rijden", "gereden", "vliegen", "gevlogen"], ["kiezen", "gekozen", "winnen", "gewonnen"], ["laten", "gelaten", "trekken", "getrokken"]] },
        { "type": "ex", "nl": "Ik heb een nieuwe fiets gekocht.", "en": "I bought a new bicycle." },
        { "type": "ex", "nl": "De trein is net vertrokken.", "en": "The train has just left." },
        { "type": "tip", "text": "*Lopen* and *rijden* are like *fietsen*: **hebben** for the activity (*Ik heb een uur gelopen*), **zijn** when there is a goal (*Ik ben naar huis gelopen*)." }
      ],
