    {
      "id": "a2.g02.l4", "title": "Prefixes: ge- or not?", "kind": "grammar",
      "explain": [
        { "type": "p", "text": "Verbs with an **unstressed prefix** (**be-, ge-, her-, ont-, ver-, er-**) get **no ge-** in the participle: *betalen → betaald*, *vertellen → verteld*, *ontmoeten → ontmoet*, *bezoeken → bezocht*. The same -t/-d rule applies." },
        { "type": "p", "text": "A **separable verb** puts **ge-** between the prefix and the verb: *opbellen → opgebeld*, *meenemen → meegenomen*, *uitnodigen → uitgenodigd*. Verbs ending in **-eren** do take ge-: *studeren → gestudeerd*." },
        { "type": "table", "head": ["infinitive", "participle", "type"], "rows": [["betalen", "betaald", "be-"], ["vertellen", "verteld", "ver-"], ["ontmoeten", "ontmoet", "ont-"], ["opbellen", "opgebeld", "separable"], ["meenemen", "meegenomen", "separable"], ["opstaan", "opgestaan (is)", "separable"], ["studeren", "gestudeerd", "-eren"]] },
        { "type": "ex", "nl": "Ik heb de rekening betaald.", "en": "I paid the bill." },
        { "type": "ex", "nl": "Zij heeft ons uitgenodigd.", "en": "She invited us." },
        { "type": "tip", "text": "In the sentence the separable verb is one word again at the end: *Ik heb mijn zus opgebeld.*" }
      ],
      "items": [
